      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Why university years are so demanding</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">A lot changes at once: the city, the language, the friends, the money, and the responsibility of managing your own time. Things your family used to carry — meals, sleep, what to do when you fall ill, rent, residence paperwork — land on one person. Then the academic calendar arrives on top of it. None of it has to go badly for you to be exhausted; it is enough that all of it moves at once.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">If you came here from abroad, there is another layer. You may be the person in your family who went overseas, which makes it harder to say that things are hard. And Famagusta is small — your social life runs through your classes, so when a friendship or a relationship ends you still sit in the same room the next morning.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Struggling in these conditions is not a personal failing. But when the struggle settles in — when sleep, appetite and the will to go to class don't come back for weeks — that is worth talking about.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">The three things students bring up most</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Exam anxiety</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Anxiety rarely takes the knowledge away — it makes the knowledge hard to reach in the room. That is where the blank feeling in front of the paper comes from, even after weeks of studying. We look separately at how the anxiety shows up in your body, how the night before goes, and how you study. See also: <a href="/en/blog/coping-with-exam-anxiety.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">coping with exam anxiety</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Procrastination and losing motivation</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Putting things off is usually not laziness. It is avoiding the possibility of failing: not starting also postpones the risk of a bad result. Making that loop visible tends to help more than adding hours to your week. If the tiredness has been there since midterms, our piece on <a href="/en/blog/burnout-syndrome.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">burnout</a> may sound familiar.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Loneliness and homesickness</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">In the first months everyone else seems to have found their people already, and social media makes that impression larger than it is. Homesickness is not only missing a person — it is losing the sense of being known. We work on how that sense gets rebuilt somewhere new. For <a href="/en/blog/mental-health-international-students-famagusta.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">international students</a> this often takes longer than one semester.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY STUDENTS CHOOSE US -->
    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
